{"summary":"Accept the redundant-breakdown refactor and the habitat-invariant comment. Reject the root-demand comment as written because it would add an unsupported calibration claim instead of a clear, neutral explanation.","votes":[{"amendment_id":"fr-1-f5cd3c88","decision":"accept","rationale":"`engine.rs` computes `substrate_oxygenation_breakdown()` for tracing and `step_substrate_zones()` immediately recomputes it for application, so the same state pays for two full breakdowns and two habitat-registry builds. Returning the breakdown from `step_substrate_zones()` or otherwise reusing one computed value removes needless work and keeps the traced values aligned with the values actually applied."},{"amendment_id":"fr-1-1c7ab74f","decision":"reject","rationale":"The current comment already explains the intended model split: rooted oxygenation is handled as a separate additive ROL bonus rather than inside the base diffusive demand term. The proposed extra comment goes further and asserts that the default `rol_rate_cm_per_g` is a literature-calibrated net parameter that already absorbs root respiration, which is not established by the code or existing docs and could mislead future maintainers. If this asymmetry needs attention, it should be fixed in code or documented more neutrally."},{"amendment_id":"fr-1-15dd3c0b","decision":"accept","rationale":"`substrate_root_activity_factor()` relies on an implicit invariant from the habitat registry: `SubstrateSurface` currently includes both the footprint top surface and the oxic interstitial area, so subtracting the footprint is how the code isolates rootable grain-matrix area. An inline comment makes that dependency explicit and lowers the chance that a future habitat-formula refactor silently breaks the calculation."}]}